WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.694
Rahul built a placement bot. He asks it

2
00:00:02.728 --> 00:00:05.375
to explain binary search, then watches a spinner

3
00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:07.245
for six long seconds.

4
00:00:07.595 --> 00:00:10.811
Same bot, same model, with streaming switched on:

5
00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:13.818
the first word shows up almost at once.

6
00:00:14.168 --> 00:00:16.722
The model isn't any faster. It writes one

7
00:00:16.757 --> 00:00:18.789
token at a time either way, and the

8
00:00:18.835 --> 00:00:21.273
last word lands at the same moment.

9
00:00:21.623 --> 00:00:23.910
What changed is the time to first token.

10
00:00:24.073 --> 00:00:26.731
That's the clock your users actually feel.

11
00:00:27.081 --> 00:00:29.682
Here's the path. The browser sends one request

12
00:00:29.705 --> 00:00:32.514
to your FastAPI backend, which calls the model

13
00:00:32.561 --> 00:00:34.511
with stream set to true.

14
00:00:34.861 --> 00:00:37.009
Every chunk that comes back is yielded into

15
00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:39.586
a streaming response, so it leaves your server

16
00:00:39.644 --> 00:00:40.852
immediately.

17
00:00:41.202 --> 00:00:43.814
On the wire it's Server-Sent Events: a line

18
00:00:43.849 --> 00:00:46.926
starting with data, then a blank line. Plain

19
00:00:46.995 --> 00:00:48.075
HTTP.

20
00:00:48.425 --> 00:00:50.944
Wrap each token in JSON, because a token

21
00:00:51.002 --> 00:00:53.429
can contain a newline, and a raw newline

22
00:00:53.475 --> 00:00:54.927
breaks the message.

23
00:00:55.277 --> 00:00:58.191
In the browser, fetch reads the stream. One

24
00:00:58.238 --> 00:01:00.234
read can hold three events, or half of

25
00:01:00.327 --> 00:01:01.221
one.

26
00:01:01.571 --> 00:01:03.487
So keep a buffer, split on the blank

27
00:01:03.556 --> 00:01:06.029
line, and add each token to the page.

28
00:01:06.379 --> 00:01:09.432
After you deploy, test it again. A proxy

29
00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.720
that buffers will hand over the whole answer

30
00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:13.020
in one lump.

31
00:01:13.370 --> 00:01:16.122
So remember: streaming doesn't cut total time or

32
00:01:16.191 --> 00:01:18.374
cost. It cuts the wait before the first

33
00:01:18.432 --> 00:01:19.361
word.

34
00:01:19.711 --> 00:01:22.730
Next lesson: cost, latency and caching.
